      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "add whatsapp outbound token diagnostics",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nOpenClaw can look successful when WhatsApp Web accepts a proactive 1:1 send but does not actually deliver it to a cold contact.\n\nThat is bad because the agent may believe a message was sent while the recipient never receives it. This PR makes that state easier to diagnose and avoids known PN/LID routing misses before send.\n\nAI-assisted: yes, Codex helped update this branch and evidence.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nWhatsApp/Baileys now stores and routes many direct contacts through LID JIDs. The old outbound path could trust stale local PN-to-LID files or miss the Baileys LID lookup path entirely.\n\nThis update keeps the fix local to the WhatsApp plugin:\n\n- resolve phone-number targets through Baileys `getLIDForPN(...)` first\n- fall back to a full-PN-JID USync lookup when needed\n- refresh stale forward and reverse LID mapping files\n- check trusted-contact token state for direct 1:1 sends\n- keep token diagnostics best-effort during reconnect windows\n- forward the needed socket query and diagnostic methods through the timeout adapter\n- bound live lookup and diagnostic calls with the same socket timeout used by sends\n\n## User Impact\n\nProactive WhatsApp sends should be less likely to go to a sender-only or wrong PN/LID target when Baileys already knows the contact LID.\n\nWhen WhatsApp has an active reachout timelock and no trusted-contact token, OpenClaw now fails before `sendMessage` with a clearer error instead of letting the send look accepted but invisible.\n\nIf the socket is reconnecting or a lookup stalls, the new diagnostics do not block the existing send retry path forever.\n\n## Evidence\n\nCurrent head: `1a89907ab0e22224636fbcb226ce5ab1b5101bc3`\n\nFocused validation after rebasing on current `origin/main`:\n\n```text\n$ node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.extension-whatsapp.config.ts extensions/whatsapp/src/inbound/send-api.test.ts extensions/whatsapp/src/socket-timing.test.ts\nTest Files  2 passed (2)\nTests       55 passed (55)\n```\n\n```text\n$ node scripts/run-tsgo.mjs -p test/tsconfig/tsconfig.extensions.test.json --incremental --tsBuildInfoFile .artifacts/tsgo-cache/extensions-test.tsbuildinfo\n# exit code 0\n```\n\n```text\n$ node scripts/run-oxlint.mjs extensions/whatsapp/src/inbound/monitor.ts extensions/whatsapp/src/inbound/send-api.ts extensions/whatsapp/src/inbound/send-api.test.ts extensions/whatsapp/src/socket-timing.ts extensions/whatsapp/src/socket-timing.test.ts\n# exit code 0\n```\n\n```text\n$ pnpm --config.verify-deps-before-run=false test:extension whatsapp\nTest Files  87 passed (87)\nTests       1039 passed (1039)\n```\n\n```text\n$ git diff --check\n# exit code 0\n```\n\n```text\n$ codex review --base origin/main\nI did not find a discrete regression in the changed WhatsApp outbound LID/token diagnostics paths.\n```\n\nThe focused tests cover:\n\n- existing authDir PN-to-LID mapping is used for sendMessage\n- Baileys `getLIDForPN(...)` is preferred before direct USync\n- direct USync uses the full PN JID rather than only the phone value\n- PN-JID recipients return the discovered LID directly\n- stale local LID mappings are overwritten by the live lookup result\n- active reachout timelock without trusted-contact token fails before sendMessage\n- missing token diagnostics do not block when reachout timelock is inactive\n- reconnect-window token lookup failures do not block the send retry path\n- timeout adapter forwards the optional query and diagnostic socket methods\n\nNot yet run: live WhatsApp delivery proof with a real account/contact.\n",
        "branch": "whatsapp-outbound-token-diagnostics",
